[[Start Quiz->Q1]]

:: Q1
Which statement best explains why humans can survive much longer without food than would be predicted from glycogen stores alone?

<<checkbox "$q1_A" false true>> A. Glycogen stores increase during fasting
<<checkbox "$q1_B" false true>> B. Most tissues can switch to fatty acid oxidation and lipid stores contain much more energy than glycogen
<<checkbox "$q1_C" false true>> C. Proteins are the main long-term energy reserve
<<checkbox "$q1_D" false true>> D. The brain exclusively oxidises fatty acids during fasting

<<button "Submit">><<goto "F1">><</button>>

:: F1
<<set _correct=0>><<set _incorrect=0>><<set _missed=[]>><<set _wrong=[]>>
<<if $q1_B>><<set _correct +=1>><<else>><<run _missed.push("B. Most tissues can switch to fatty acid oxidation and lipid stores contain much more energy than glycogen")>><</if>>
<<if $q1_A>><<set _incorrect +=1>><<run _wrong.push("A. Glycogen stores increase during fasting")>><</if>><<if $q1_C>><<set _incorrect +=1>><<run _wrong.push("C. Proteins are the main long-term energy reserve")>><</if>><<if $q1_D>><<set _incorrect +=1>><<run _wrong.push("D. The brain exclusively oxidises fatty acids during fasting")>><</if>>
<<set $totalCorrect += _correct>><<set $totalPossible += 1>><<set $totalIncorrect += _incorrect>>
Model answer: B. Most tissues can switch to fatty acid oxidation and lipid stores contain much more energy than glycogen.

Humans store relatively little carbohydrate as glycogen, but adipose triacylglycerol reserves contain far more chemical energy. During fasting, many tissues increase fatty acid oxidation, reducing glucose demand. Glycogen stores do not increase during fasting, proteins are not the preferred long-term energy reserve, and the brain cannot exclusively oxidise fatty acids because they cross the blood-brain barrier poorly. Ketone bodies partially support brain metabolism during prolonged fasting.

